#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# dependencies = ["boto3", "h5py>=3.0.0,<4", "numpy>=1.24.0,<2.5", "pyyaml"]
# ///

"""
ant-ingest — manual ingestion from S3 __incoming/ to the catalog.

The h5ad deposits go through the same pipeline as the S3 watch route
(``lib/ingest_pipeline.py``, ``bin/ingest/ant-ingest-watch``; design:
``doc/DESIGN_dataset_ingestion.md``, "S3 watch route"), run once by hand:

  1. Scan s3://<bucket>/__incoming/ and group it into datasets
     (``__incoming/<data_project>/[<subfolder>/]<basename>.h5ad`` + ``.h5ad.mus``).
  2. Pull each h5ad and its sidecar into the work dir
     (``config.get_ingest_work_dir()/manual``, never inside the store) and verify
     the sha256 the sidecar declares.
  3. New dataset: write the catalogue ``.md`` (non-destructive fill: sha256,
     n_obs, layer stats, obsnum/obscat defaults, version 1.0.0 and a first
     changelog entry; seeds from ``static/<basename>.md`` / ``static/index.md``).
     Existing dataset: a new version (minor when the h5ad changed, patch for a
     sidecar-only change) with a changelog entry; the previous version is kept
     under ``<group>/_versions/<basename>/<version>/`` and on S3.
  4. ``ant-convert`` in the work dir, then ``ant-ingest-check`` (JSON report;
     errors and held reasons block, warnings pass).
  5. Atomic placement into the store (an update swaps under
     ``/admin/release`` + ``/admin/restore``), static/ promotion, rescan.
  6. Push the h5ad, sidecar, duckdb and layer files to the catalog root on
     S3, re-stamp ``s3_*`` in the sidecar.
  7. Record the consumed __incoming/ keys in
     ``<ingest_state_dir>/pending_prune.json`` for ``ant-ingest prune``.

DE-only deposits (``__incoming/<dp>/de/<folder>/<file>`` naming an existing
dataset) still go through ``ant-de-ingest`` here.

Subcommands:
  ant-ingest                         # pull + run end-to-end
  ant-ingest --dry-run               # show what would happen
  ant-ingest --filter <data_project> # restrict scan to one project
  ant-ingest prune [--dry-run]       # remove the work files + S3 __incoming/ keys
                                     # recorded by past runs
  ant-ingest prune --include-catalog-orphans
  ant-ingest purge <db_id> [--keep N] [--dry-run] [--yes]
  ant-ingest purge --all --keep N    # remove archived versions (local + S3)
  ant-ingest purge --all --local-only --verified   # local copies whose S3 copy is verified
  ant-ingest purge <db_id> --superseded-minor      # predecessors of verified patch/minor versions
  (versions an artefact's made_from or a facet result names stay unless --force)

Non-destructive guarantee: the default command never deletes anything on S3.
``prune`` and ``purge`` are the only commands that call S3 DeleteObject; both
show what they remove first (``--dry-run``) and ``purge`` asks for
confirmation. The S3 watch route (``ant ingest-watch``) deletes the consumed
incoming objects itself after a verified publication.
"""
